Hoisting Behavior
1. var
    Hoisted ✅ (moved to the top of the scope in memory)
    Initialized with undefined immediately in the Creation Phase
    Can be accessed before its actual line of code (but will give undefined until assigned).

    console.log(a); // undefined ✅
    var a = 10;
    console.log(a); // 10

2. let
    Hoisted ✅ (the name is placed in memory during Creation Phase)
    Not initialized immediately (stays in the Temporal Dead Zone until its line of code is executed)
    Accessing before initialization → ReferenceError

    console.log(b); // ❌ ReferenceError (TDZ)
    let b = 20;
    console.log(b); // 20

3. const
    Hoisted ✅ (same as let)
    Not initialized until its declaration line is executed
    Must be initialized at declaration — otherwise, SyntaxError
    Accessing before initialization → ReferenceError (TDZ)

    console.log(c); // ❌ ReferenceError (TDZ)
    const c = 30;
    console.log(c); // 30

Hoisting Table Recap
    Keyword	Hoisted?	Initialized in Creation Phase?	TDZ?	Default Value Before Init
    var	✅ Yes	✅ Yes (undefined)	❌ No	undefined
    let	✅ Yes	❌ No	✅ Yes	N/A (error if accessed)
    const	✅ Yes	❌ No	✅ Yes	N/A (error if accessed)

💡 Key takeaway:

All variables (var, let, const) are hoisted in terms of memory allocation,
but only var gets an immediate default value (undefined) in the creation phase.

let and const stay in the Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ) until execution reaches their declaration line.

